<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!-- No package references, and no project references either. That is the rule rather than what
       happened to be true on the first day, and an architecture test asserts the list stays empty.

       This is the project a front end references. A Blazor WebAssembly client ships every assembly it
       references to the browser, so a dependency taken here is a dependency downloaded by every
       visitor - and the application project references EF, a database provider and whatever vendor
       SDKs the solution needs. None of that can be allowed to reach a client, which is the whole
       reason this project exists rather than the UI referencing the application directly. D-38 records
       it.

       It is also why a response record lives here rather than nested in its holder. The holder keeps
       the query, the validator, the handler and the registration - every decision the operation
       makes. What moves out is a flat record with no behaviour in it. -->

</Project>
